## Automatic planning at implementation MR creation

When a predecessor implementation MR first opens against `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`,
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} verifies its source/target/candidate and automatically starts
provisional planning for the earliest eligible task with an existing ready Goal/Spec.
Draft implementation MRs qualify; planning-only/placeholder/probe MRs do not.
Do not wait for CI/Duo, ready status, merge or final acceptance to begin planning.
Reuse the existing planner, honor owner stops and record the MR baseline and pending
gates. Reconcile relevant revisions and integrated inputs before execution;
closure without integration requires reassessment. Integration is a fallback
checkpoint if initial discovery was missed, never a duplicate dispatch.
Inspection/Plan artifacts only; no code/tests/builds/shared-stack use or early
execution. Plan review, bindings, budgets, owner decisions and resource/release
gates remain; implementation overlap follows the release policy below. See [planning policy](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-planning-after-integration).

## Automatic reassessment and execution release

After reviewed predecessor source is integrated, {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} reassesses
and may release isolated implementation of the next approved task while predecessor
post-merge affected local qualification/closeout runs. Verify Plan/input/scope, owner decisions, other dependencies,
budget and resources; record the exact predecessor revision and pending qualification.
No extra owner prompt. Predecessor final ACCEPT still gates successor integration
and final acceptance. Material changes require bounded Plan updates/review;
unresolved security/architecture/contracts, explicit holds, native permissions and
delivery barriers stop affected work. Preserve unaffected work and scope any
predecessor repair under its existing implementation owner/ledger, in parallel
with successor work; do not transfer responsibility to successor workers by default.
See [release policy](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-reassessment-and-execution-release).

## Supervisor progress publication

Routine acceptance and cleanup closeout records use the supervisor's direct publication route to the configured integration branch. The coordinator hands back the Result, attributable qualification evidence and cleanup receipt with exact source references; the supervisor reviews them, records the acceptance disposition and imports/publishes the completed Result, review, PROGRESS and related closeout records in scoped commits. Do not create a separate acceptance, Result-only or cleanup-receipt MR, request another automated review or launch CI solely to publish those records. The implementer receives no direct integration-branch push or self-acceptance authority. An explicit owner instruction or an actual branch-protection requirement can select an MR instead; report that reason. Actual code or executable configuration repairs retain the normal source-MR workflow. Preserve review, evidence, cleanup and native permission gates; publication alone is not ACCEPT.

Supervisor auto-publication: `{{SUPERVISOR_AUTO_PUBLISH}}` (enabled by default). When enabled, {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}},
after completing a PROGRESS update, commits and pushes that update and its associated management records (imported
Results and status, reviews, decisions) to `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` from the main integration checkout without another
owner confirmation. It stages exact owned paths, excludes secrets and machine-local logs, fetches and reconciles
first, never force-pushes, and reports a conflict, protected-branch rule or permission gate instead of bypassing it.
Publication is not review or ACCEPT. {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} delivers Results and status and keeps its task-branch and
merge-request workflow; this grants no direct implementer push. The owner may disable it (`false`) for the repository
or for an explicit task or session; record the override. It is an agent workflow, not a watcher, hook or scheduler.

## Apply workflow updates to live repository guidance

When the supervisor updates Restruct SDLC workflow rules, it also updates the
corresponding live governance files in the adopting repository in the same change
batch, then commits and pushes to that repository's configured integration branch
under its publication policy. Updating reusable references or adoption templates
alone is insufficient. Preserve repository-specific settings and existing task
bindings; record any conflict or explicit owner deferral rather than silently
leaving the live guidance stale. This applies to every adopting repository.


## Supervisor checkout, delegated review and relay

{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} normally works in the main integration checkout. Prefer bounded subagents for Spec
drafting/research and formal Plan/Result reviews; reviewers are read-only by default, and the supervisor owns the
disposition. When a new watcher notice or owner-requested status check shows a review-ready Plan or Result, start
the delegated review without another owner prompt: verify the exact artifact and phase, skip a duplicate review of
unchanged bytes, and review directly (recording why) when delegation is unavailable. Record the outcome, then relay
it with the remaining gates to the verified coordinator and verify transport receipt. The owner may interrupt or
redirect; mandatory escalations and owner merge authority remain. No additional watcher, transport or early
implementation authority is added. The session-handoff watcher already polls the coordinator and delivers notices;
the supervisor does not poll sessions or ping the watcher unless the owner specifically asks for a bounded status
check. Verify linked repository records on new notices. Send needed coordinator decisions or requests immediately using nonblocking delivery.
Continue supervisor work without waiting for acknowledgment or polling the transcript;
the watcher delivers responses asynchronously. For a bounded delegated review, keep the supervisor turn open
with the harness's asynchronous
agent wait/notification interface (the owner may steer meanwhile). Finalize, record and relay the decision before the
final reply. Use bounded waits without repeated inference-based polling; waiting itself uses no model tokens, but
reviewer work and result processing do. An ended turn may not wake automatically, so say so rather than promising it.
Interrupting the supervisor's turn alone is not stop-all: on resuming, inspect child status, keep completed findings
and authorized running work, and do not cancel or re-dispatch automatically. An explicit stop-all or a conflicting
scope, security or owner direction governs; never assume a client cancellation reached child agents.

## Coordinator and workers: naming, dispatch and relocation gate

1. `0-agent-<id>` is reserved for the single active persistent coordinator; its existing agent ID is kept when
   available, and no worker uses the `0-` prefix.
2. Exactly one coordinator is active, verified by complete discovery and the owner-selected pin
   (`pinned-live-verified`). Unknown or ambiguous discovery never justifies another coordinator.
3. A directory name grants no authority, identity or routing; discovery, the verified pin and recorded task
   ownership do.
4. Prefer `<task-id>-agent-<agent-id>-<role>` when supported (for example `g002-agent-<id>-impl`, `-plan`, `-check`).
   {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} workers live under `.claude/worktrees/`; {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} workers that need isolation
   live under `.codex/worktrees/`. Read-only reviewers need no checkout.
5. Before dispatch, record task/role and preferred path if supported; otherwise use native isolated-worker creation.
6. Before task writes, verify actual Git path, branch, HEAD, agent/session owner and task/role. Wrong ownership,
   branch or unsafe isolation fails; a generated basename alone does not.
7. Treat unsupported Claude task/role naming as a harness bug/limitation and continue with its native `agent-<id>`
   worktree, recording the actual task mapping. No task-specific exception or naming approval/prompt is required.
   Never delay authorized work, duplicate sessions or bypass native permissions merely to enforce a name.
8. Relocation defers by default: no move beneath a live process, and no restart or duplicate session for naming. A
   move needs complete discovery, positively verified stopped ownership, separately proven absence of cwd and
   open-file users, a quiet state and a recorded supported same-UUID restart.
9. Only then: `git worktree move`, verify, restart the same UUID, reconcile path and pin provenance. Any failure
   stops.

Owner attribution: relay an actual owner decision as "The owner explicitly authorized this action; {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} is
relaying that authorization", naming the action and decision record. A relayed authorization never overrides a native
permission rejection or a gate that needs direct owner action; report it instead. Observer notices carry no authority.

Details and the full preconditions: [coordination](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md).
Session discovery, routing, receipts and closeout archiving use
[claude-session-handoff](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/claude-session-handoff/SKILL.md) and the local
[verification record](docs/agents/CLAUDE-SESSION-HANDOFF.md); supervision resumes with
[repository-supervisor](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/repository-supervisor/SKILL.md).

## Combining management and foundation skills

When used with `saas-foundation` or a similar renderer, `sdlc-management` supersedes only overlapping management
process. Renderer dry runs, resolved-input review, informed authorization, conflict safeguards, technical checks and
canonical decisions stay; foundation stages become Goal and acceptance inputs; the renderer's plan is not an
Implementation Plan; unresolved technical conflicts escalate.

## Execution policy

The supervisor states the risk tier (direct, ordinary, or security/data/architecture) in each Goal. After Plan
approval, or within an explicitly authorized direct-tier Goal/Spec without a separate Plan, the implementer works
autonomously within scope; agents never select a lighter tier themselves. Stop and escalate: architecture contradictions, scope
expansion, security-model changes, destructive migrations, public contract changes, new major infrastructure or
vendors, irreversible data-model decisions.

Budgets follow the [budget policy](management/BUDGET_POLICY.md); the default class is {{DEFAULT_BUDGET}}. Effort
recommendations by role: coordinator {{COORDINATOR_EFFORT}} for orchestration, status and handoffs; planning workers
(and a coordinator that plans) {{PLANNING_EFFORT}}; implementation workers {{IMPLEMENTATION_EFFORT}}. Set worker
effort explicitly through supported controls; verify actual or inherited values and record unverifiable effort as
unknown. The owner may raise complex debugging or security work to xhigh.

## Milestone observer activation

Observer auto-activation: `{{OBSERVER_AUTO_ACTIVATE}}` (enabled by default; independent of supervisor publication).
When enabled, once the coordinator pin is verified and the existing supervisor UUID is explicitly bound, the
coordinator workflow starts the companion observer (`watch.py bind`, then `start`, 30-second default) without
another enablement question. Missing, ambiguous, unverified or retired bindings leave it inactive and reported.
There is no name guessing, session creation or permission change. A repeated start is idempotent, and `false`
opts out. Notices, including the transcript-size advisory, carry no authority.

## Validation and integration

### Default parallel scheduling

Record high/low priority and its source/rationale when authoring the Goal/Spec.
Honor an explicit owner choice; if unspecified, the supervisor may determine it
or ask when needed, normally defaulting to high without a routine hold. The owner
should identify low-priority work. Plans target 3–5 parallel implementation workers
for high or 1–3 for low whenever independent work, exclusive write ownership,
native/resource capacity and budget permit; exclude planner/coordinator/reviewers.
Record
worker counts by phase, responsibilities, paths, inputs, dependencies, integration
owner and resource reservations in the existing Plan. Record concrete constraints
when fewer workers than the priority target are feasible. The planner may remain one
agent. At implementation kickoff, verify readiness and dispatch the accepted
Plan's ready workers, following its allocation/sequence; record actual deviations
and constraints in the Result, with normal review for material changes. Low-
priority work may use one worker within its 1–3 target and yield capacity to
the current high-priority implementation. Keep one task owner and one writer per
artifact; use approved delegation and isolation and reclaim finished workers.
A failure holds that lane and its dependents; unrelated
safe lanes may continue. Serialize shared files, dependent changes, mutable fixtures
and resources where locks or configured limits require it. Private database/browser
runs share the configured pool; image builds remain sequential. Parallel scheduling
preserves task-specific sequencing, frozen sources and all acceptance, review,
qualification and owner gates. Apply active-task changes at safe handoffs,
preserving Plan history and frozen runs. Use existing coordinator facilities and
records. See [parallel scheduling](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#default-to-safe-parallel-work-lanes).

Before each batch, fetch and assess the exact delta for validation and execution-authority impact. If irrelevant
to both, record the target range/candidate/reason and defer rebase/input reconstruction. Otherwise reconcile;
unknown impact follows reconciliation. Always reconcile before final qualification and MR opening; keep resource
checks and existing approved contracts. No blanket folder exemption. Expensive CI meta-tests are selected by
actual CI/harness/control dependencies; retain required application/security and hosted path-selected coverage.

For new work, Git commit and path bind committed code and reviewed management inputs; the compact Result records
tested commits, commands, outcomes and relevant runtime inputs. Relevant dirty/untracked changes must be identified.
Extra snapshots/hashes cover only inputs Git does not capture; preserve existing approved bindings and secret-safe
handling. Do not create parallel source inventories or retain whole checkout copies by default. See
[Git-first provenance](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#git-first-provenance).

Follow the [branch workflow](docs/agents/BRANCH-WORKFLOW.md). Before every local batch: fetch, assess the delta,
reconcile unless the documented irrelevant-delta exception applies, and run the environment check
({{PRE_BATCH_ENV_CHECK}}). Batches finish, fix, rerun, then advance within each dependency chain; independent safe
batches may proceed in parallel. Manual feedback precedes final qualification.
With the default `local-after-merge` placement, affected local suites run on the exact merged integration SHA; full
local runs on the integration tip are owner-requested or supervisor-recommended, not a routine feature gate. Final validation placement: `{{FINAL_VALIDATION_PLACEMENT}}`; canceled post-merge pipeline satisfies the gate:
`{{CANCELED_PIPELINE_SATISFIES}}`. Integrate through reviewed merge requests to `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` with
`{{MR_TOOL}}`; merge only with owner authorization. Protected branches: {{PROTECTED_BRANCHES}}.

## Closeout and risks

Record temporary-resource ownership at creation in the existing inventory, including exact container/image/volume
IDs. Reuse existing cleanup with one scoped pass and a compact receipt; hold uncertain items and finish the verified
remainder. Unrelated host activity does not block cleanup. On native refusal, report once for owner handling, with no
bypass or repeated approval relays. Preserve required active-run gates. See the closeout reference for details.

Acceptance and cleanup are separate; verify integrated content, unique work and live ownership before removing
anything. After verified terminal closeout, preserve compact acceptance/provenance/failure records and necessary
proof, then purge redundant task-owned local validation logs, traces/videos, reports and scratch by default.
Default durable evidence to committed records and CI artifacts. Local raw files are temporary;
purge closed-task bulk/duplicate downloads, keeping only explicit necessary-proof exceptions with release conditions.
Protect active/dependent/held evidence; verify exact path ownership/containment and no live use; record retained
locations, purged paths/count/bytes, holds and reclaimed space. Do not delete session transcripts/delivery archives
or committed history under this rule. Archive session records before pruning. Compact PROGRESS into dated archives with working links. Every epic closeout requires
a recorded compaction disposition: archive superseded updates, record no changes needed, or explain an
active-binding deferral and its next safe checkpoint. Compaction alone needs no test rerun. Record
performance and other risks in [RISKS](docs/RISKS.md).

## Model-aware effort override — 2026-10-15

Default to Fable 5.1 high for planning and test corrections, including failure
diagnosis and corrective test/fix work; use xhigh selectively for especially
hard work. Default to Opus 5.5 high for implementation and routine test execution.
Default the Claude coordinator to Sonnet 5.5 high; the owner may choose Opus 5.5
high for coordinator work. If the owner selects Opus 5.5 for planning or test
corrections/diagnosis, use xhigh; routine implementation, test execution and
coordination stay high. This overrides generic role-effort defaults for those
models/phases. Honor owner choices, verify actual settings or report unknown,
and apply only at the next phase/safe boundary without restarting completed work,
interrupting a batch or resetting budgets. Prose does not configure the runtime.


## Fable weekly-limit fallback — owner direction, 2026-10-15

When Fable's weekly allowance is confirmed exhausted, automatically use Opus 5.5
**xhigh for planning and test-failure diagnosis/corrections** until Fable resets.
Opus implementation and ordinary test execution/reporting remain **high**.
Coordinator default remains Sonnet 5.5 high, with Opus 5.5 high available by
owner choice. This standing fallback needs no repeat owner approval; honor an explicit
owner override. Record the limit, fallback model/effort and actual setting (or
unknown) in the existing Result/dispatch record. A generic tool error is not
proof of a model limit.

Use supported controls at the next safe boundary, preserving the existing task,
artifacts, checkout, bindings, reviews and attempt count. Do not interrupt a
running validation batch, redo completed work, reset budgets or weaken gates.
If native switching is unavailable or refused, report the blocker once; do not
bypass permissions. Once Fable's allowance reset is verified, return to its normal
model/effort defaults at the next safe phase boundary, without restarting work or
repeatedly probing the allowance. This is model selection only, not scope,
execution-release or budget-extension authority.


Use the shared [validation economy](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#validation-economy-and-recurring-failures),
[automatic routine transitions](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#continue-routine-transitions-without-owner-nudges),
[concise messages](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#concise-operational-messages) and
[closeout receipt](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#routine-closeout-receipt).
Preserve explicit owner limits and native permissions; reuse existing records/tools.

Container-produced retained artifacts must be removable by the host operator;
use [artifact ownership](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#container-artifact-ownership)
at creation rather than recurring privileged cleanup.

## Reuse existing coverage in Plans

Inspect relevant existing tests first. In the normal Plan/AC mapping cite test paths
or check IDs as reuse/extend/new, with the gap or distinct boundary for new tests.
Prefer existing fixtures; no cloned suite or new check merely for this Goal. Keep
required regressions, local/hosted/security coverage and current evidence binding.
See [planning coverage reuse](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#reuse-existing-coverage-during-planning).

At handoffs/closeout or when old conversation history dominates context, follow
[coordinator context compaction](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-context-compaction-at-safe-boundaries).
Preserve durable state, session/observer identity and running batches; no per-turn compaction or new monitor.
Use [bounded worker handoffs](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#worker-context-and-phase-handoffs)
when obsolete context warrants them; default checkpoint target **150,000 active context tokens**,
upper handoff threshold **200,000**. Prepare concise transfer state around 150k at a safe boundary;
checkpointing alone does not force immediate rotation or review. Preserve budgets/evidence;
no full-transcript fork, mandatory per-phase rotation or test restart.

Use [targeted reruns and attributable coverage](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#targeted-reruns-and-attributable-coverage-default) by default:
rerun failed cases and affected dependencies, reuse unchanged passes, preserve
failed/canceled history, and overlap frozen-source review with independent tests.
Keep required hosted/atomic evidence and final acceptance gates.

Local validation starts with [focused executable selections](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#focused-selection-before-the-first-run),
including first/final runs. Check actual command scope; broad suites require a
recorded technical reason and supervisor disposition before launch, not convenience.
Reuse approved exceptions without repeated prompts; mandatory hosted gates remain.

Apply [early risk review and short correction loops](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#early-risk-review-and-short-correction-loops)
inside existing planning/review: changed trust boundaries, risky slice, caller/pin
inventory and consolidated corrections. No extra stage, framework or active-work restart.

Use the existing Plan to separate settled decisions from unproven assumptions and
schedule the smallest proof of the highest-risk assumption early in approved
implementation. Estimate coding effort separately from build/test elapsed time
and review/queue waits; give executable selectors, dependencies and resource
reservations, using available timings or honest estimates/unknowns. Material scope
additions need a bounded impact delta and applicable approval. A failed proof or
material overrun needs a compact Result checkpoint and bounded correction; apply
only to remaining work at a safe boundary, without a new reporting framework.

For [coordinator checkpoints and retirement](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-checkpoints-and-retirement),
use 150k active context for checkpointing and 200k for compaction-or-handoff
assessment, not a hard kill. Prefer supported compaction. Replace only with
recorded creation authority, verified native cutover and explicit observer rebind;
preserve active agents, budgets and evidence. Documentation alone does not
implement automatic replacement or retire a live coordinator.

Apply [session continuity](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#supervisor-session-continuity)
to the supervisor as well as the coordinator, using actual model/harness limits
and compaction first. Honor recorded task/phase transition exceptions; do not
force live replacement or assume delegates/thread bindings transfer themselves.


## Continuous resource accountability — owner direction, 2026-10-22

Use explicit project/run ownership labels for every newly created exclusively
owned Docker object that supports them, through Compose and direct commands.
Set `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` per stack/run; direct creators need equivalent explicit
labels. Discover by labels, then verify exact identity/lifecycle using the existing
compact run registry. Preserve anonymous/shared exceptions and reboot recovery;
no extra cleanup manifest, label-only deletion or relabeling of active bound work.

Cleanup is non-negotiable throughout execution, not only at final acceptance.
Record every container/image/volume/network, worktree and temporary path in the
existing task/run inventory at allocation, including nested/partial creations;
update ownership and removal as they happen. Reconcile actual resources before
startup/resume/dispatch, at each worker handback and batch end, and on failure,
cancellation and acceptance. Reclaim finished worker trees and disposable scratch
as soon as the handback and unique source are preserved. Stop/reap creators before
cleanup and verify absence afterwards. Failed cleanup blocks new resource-consuming
work until reconciled; legitimate live/required retention must have a named owner,
reason and release condition. A crash/reboot requires recovery reconciliation;
traps alone cannot ensure cleanup. Report all resource categories and unresolved
counts in the existing receipt. Preserve credentials, native session state,
required minimal failure proof and unrelated/shared resources. This is event-driven
accounting, not coordinator polling. Repair and focus-test executable leaks before
resuming the affected workload; prose alone is insufficient. See
[continuous resource accountability](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#continuous-resource-accountability--mandatory).

## Mandatory task-acceptance cleanup — owner direction, 2026-10-17

At every final task ACCEPT, immediately remove all finished task-owned worktrees
and temporary resources under the [mandatory cleanup policy](.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#mandatory-cleanup-at-every-task-acceptance).
Unlock finished agent-tool worktrees; preserve unique commits and dirty/untracked
work before force removal when needed. Clean task-owned containers, unused images
(including attributable untagged IDs), volumes and disposable evidence without
